{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. Rectangle","reason":"A parallelogram with equal diagonals has right angles, so it is a rectangle; equal side lengths are not guaranteed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_188_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equal-diagonals-criterion","label":"Apply the rectangle criterion","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-diagonals-criterion":["cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_188"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, a rectangle that is not a square","reason":"The four points are (3,4), (4,3), (-3,-4), and (-4,-3). Opposite points form diagonals of length 10 with common midpoint (0,0), so the quadrilateral is a rectangle. Its adjacent side lengths are √2 and 7√2, so it is not a square.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"],"id":"solution:equal-bisecting-diameters:4ce33274d5a3","label":"Method 1: Use the circle's diameters","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-bisecting-diameters":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"],"id":"solve-and-check-slopes","label":"Method 2: Check the side slopes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-and-check-slopes":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h167","cnm_h168","cnm_184","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h167","cnm_h168","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, equality holds if and only if MNPQ is a rectangle","reason":"Triangulating along the two diagonals gives A≤(ab+cd)/2 and A≤(ad+bc)/2. Averaging yields A≤(a+c)(b+d)/4. Equality requires equality in both bounds, forcing all four angles to be right angles; every rectangle attains equality.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_064","cnm_p077","cnm_p089"],"id":"average-diagonal-bounds","label":"Combine area bounds from both diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"average-diagonal-bounds":["cnm_p150","cnm_065","cnm_h018","cnm_188","cnm_h013"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_065","cnm_h018","cnm_188","cnm_h013","cnm_p148","cnm_064","cnm_p077","cnm_p089"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, rectangle","reason":"Adjacent angles of a parallelogram sum to 180°. If they are equal, each is 90°, giving a rectangle; every rectangle satisfies the stated condition.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_059"],"id":"prove-both-directions","label":"Check both directions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prove-both-directions":["cnm_188","cnm_186"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_188","cnm_186","cnm_069","cnm_059"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3√5/4","reason":"The midpoints are E=(1/2,0,3/2), F=(1/2,0,0), G=(0,1,0), and H=(0,1,3/2). Opposite sides are parallel, and EF is perpendicular to FG. Their lengths are EF=3/2 and FG=√((1/2)²+1²)=√5/2, so the rectangle's area is 3√5/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h151","cnm_055","cnm_174","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"],"id":"solution:triangle-midsegments:1424da505c3b","label":"Use triangle midsegments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-midsegments":["cnm_185","cnm_h120","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_h120","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p089","cnm_h151","cnm_055","cnm_174","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. A quadrilateral with three right angles","reason":"The fourth angle is 360° - 3 × 90° = 90°, so all four angles are right angles. The other conditions do not guarantee a rectangle.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_188_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_181","cnm_186"],"id":"main","label":"Use the rectangle criteria","methodDirectNodeIds":{"main":["cnm_188","cnm_190"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_188","cnm_190","cnm_181","cnm_186"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"8","reason":"Since AC = BD, ABCD is a rectangle. Thus BC = sqrt(10² - 6²) = 8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_188_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_075"],"id":"main","label":"Recognize a rectangle and use the Pythagorean theorem","methodDirectNodeIds":{"main":["cnm_188","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_188","cnm_174","cnm_186","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"48","reason":"The diagonals bisect each other, so AC = BD = 10. The rectangle has BC = sqrt(100 - 36) = 8 and area 6 × 8 = 48.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_188_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_183","cnm_186","cnm_075"],"id":"main","label":"Find the rectangle's dimensions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"main":["cnm_188","cnm_174","c