 is a square","reason":"A rhombus is a parallelogram, so equal diagonals make it a rectangle. It therefore has four equal sides and four right angles, which define a square.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_191_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_189"],"id":"current","label":"Classify the quadrilaterals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"current":["cnm_191","cnm_188","cnm_190"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_188","cnm_190","cnm_189"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 5","reason":"The circle has 12 integer points: four axial points and eight points with coordinate magnitudes 3 and 4. Removing the two fixed vertices leaves 10 points, forming five antipodal pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1995_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"common-diagonal-circle","label":"Common diagonal circle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-diagonal-circle":["cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_188"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"right-angle-dot-product","label":"Right-angle equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-angle-dot-product":["cnm_h088","cnm_h090","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h088","cnm_h090","cnm_188"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_h173","cnm_250"],"id":"diagonal-circle","label":"Count possible diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonal-circle":["math:lattice-counting","cnm_186","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["math:lattice-counting","cnm_186","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_082","cnm_h173","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3","reason":"Because 8²+15²=17², the area is 8×15/2=60. The semiperimeter is 20, so r=60/20=3. Equivalently, r=(8+15-17)/2=3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_262"],"id":"tangent-segments","label":"Method 2: Use equal tangent segments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-segments":["cnm_264","cnm_263","cnm_188","cnm_186"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","cnm_263","cnm_188","cnm_186","cnm_177","cnm_262"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 12.25","reason":"Let AB=a and CD=c. The right-triangle relation gives BC^2=49+(c-a)^2. Since BC=a+c, subtraction yields 4ac=49, so ac=49/4=12.25.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_10_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_055","cnm_110","cnm_188"],"id":"right-triangle-and-product","label":"Construct a right triangle and subtract the squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-triangle-and-product":["cnm_174","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_148","cnm_p077","cnm_055","cnm_110","cnm_188"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: sqrt(65)","reason":"Place the chord endpoints at (-3,0), (4,0), (0,-6), and (0,2). Their perpendicular bisectors locate the center at (1/2,-2). Thus R^2=(7/2)^2+2^2=65/4, so the diameter is sqrt(65).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q46","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_188","cnm_075"],"id":"chord-midpoints","label":"Locate the center using chord midpoints","methodDirectNodeIds":{"chord-midpoints":["cnm_251","cnm_174","cnm_186"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_251","cnm_174","cnm_186","cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_188","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 7, with minimum PQ = 120/17 ≈ 7.059","reason":"Let O be the diagonal intersection. Then OA=8, OB=15, AB=17, and PQ=ON. The perpendicular from O meets AB internally, giving minimum ON=(8·15)/17=120/17, closest to 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_188","cnm_186"],"id":"rectangle-and-altitude","label":"Replace the moving segment by a distance","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rectangle-and-altitude":["cnm_189","cnm_192","cnm_065","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_189","cnm_192","cnm_065","cnm_p148","cnm_174","cnm_188","cnm_186"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (4)","reason":"The area is 2(a-b)(a+b), so (a-b)(a+b) = 8. These positive factors must have the same parity and satisfy a-b < a+b. Only (2,4) works, giving a = 3, b = 1 and a+b = 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12B_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"rectangle-side-lengths","label":"Recognize a rectangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rectangle-side-lengths":["cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_h173","cnm_p089","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_h173","cnm_p089","cnm_152"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (2)","reason":"Take E=(0,0,0), D=(2,0,0), and A=(0,2,0). Since AC²=8, the conditions allow C=(2,1,√3). The plane-parallel and right-isosceles conditions give B=(2,2,0) or B=(0,1,√3). Both have perpendicular distance 2 from DE, so the area is ½·2·2=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_133","cnm_h113"],"id":"parallel-plane-square-construction","label":"Construct a square in the plane of ABC","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-plane-square-construction":["cnm_h117","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h117","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p148","cnm_174","cnm_133","cnm_h113"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":12,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 67; possible areas are 9/5 and 5/3","reason":"The outer side is √10. After multiplying coordinates by √10 and placing the origin at the upper-left corner, the shared vertex of R is (4,3), and the long rectangle edge is 3y=x+11. Writing R's top vertex as (h,0), the rectangle conditions give (h-7)(h-8)=0. These yield actual areas 9/5 and 5/3, whose sum is 52/15. Thus m+n=67.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_10B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_174","cnm_177","cnm_183","cnm_p173","cnm_p168"],"id":"coordinates-and-pythagoras","label":"Coordinates and perpendicular sides","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinates-and-pythagoras":["cnm_084","cnm_245","cnm_186","cnm_188","cnm_224","cnm_p089","math:similar-figure-area-scaling"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_245","cnm_186","cnm_188","cnm_224","cnm_p089","math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_p077","cnm_174","cnm_177","cnm_183","cnm_p173","cnm_p168"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"cnm_188","status":"mapped","total":20}
